rname>Bernstein</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Extended somatic support for pregnant women after brain death</article-title><source>Crit Care Med</source><year>2003</year><volume>31</volume><fpage>1241</fpage><lpage>1249</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12682499</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1097/01.CCM.0000059643.45027.96</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B10"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Byrne</surname><given-names>PA</given-names></name><name><surname>O'Reilly</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Quay</surname><given-names>PM</given-names></name><name><surname>Salsich</surname><given-names>PW</given-names><suffix>Jr</suffix></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Byrne PA, Nilges RG, Potts M</surname></name></person-group><article-title>Brain death: the patient, the physician, and society</article-title><source>Beyond Brain Death</source><year>2000</year><publisher-name> The Netherlands: Kluwer Academic Publishers</publisher-name><fpage>21</fpage><lpage>89</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B11"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shewmon</surname><given-names>DA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The brain and somatic integration: insights into the standard biological rationale for equating 'brain death' with death</article-title><source>J Med Phil</source><year>2001</year><volume>26</volume><fpage>457</fpage><lpage>478</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11588655</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1076/jmep.26.5.457.3000</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B12"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Youngner</surname><given-names>SJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Bartlett</surname><given-names>ET</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Human death and high technology: the failure of the whole-brain formulations</article-title><source>Ann Intern Med</source><year>1983</year><volume>99</volume><fpage>252</fpage><lpage>258</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6881781</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Whetstine</surname><given-names>LM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>An examination of the bio-philosophical literature on the definition and criteria of death; when is dead 'dead' and why some donation after cardiac death donors are not</article-title><source>PhD thesis</source><year>2006</year><publisher-name>Duquesne University</publisher-name></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>